<http://data.silknow.org/object/ebcd749c-a187-3d45-bd22-aec2dc4bb321>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "This length was originally part of a kimono. The ground fabric is rinzu, a monochrome patterned silk that was introduced to Japan from China in the sixteenth century. The design of peonies has been created using a technique known as yuzen in which a cloth tube fitted with a metal tip is used to apply a thin ribbon of rice paste to the outlines of a drawing on the fabric. Dyes of various colours are brushed within the paste boundaries, which prevent bleeding of one colour into another.  This method has been combined with a stencil-dyeing technique called kata kanoko, which imitates tie-dyeing, and embroidery in silk and metallic threads."@en , "Tex, Japan, embroidery"@en , "Length from a kimono embroidered and dyed with design of peonies"@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/52df9de9-1693-3807-afe1-8a51e20edb0e> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/995f246e-3374-3451-8e7c-e6168dec80f7> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/3b9c195b-4448-399e-abb5-823d4da3f2e1> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/630d590b-7cfb-3fdc-8e6f-3326d0f504f4> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/44a4c580-fdc6-3824-a5ff-43fe6d6410ea> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/9da8891d-0007-3ec4-813f-f5e714241a96> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/bef22b9a-f6e9-373d-bade-1d1d674b98a5> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/7c1201d9-7a71-3c5f-8e45-9caaa98222fa> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Length from a kimono embroidered and dyed with design of peonies"@en , "This length was originally part of a kimono. The ground fabric is rinzu, a monochrome patterned silk that was introduced to Japan from China in the sixteenth century. The design of peonies has been created using a technique known as yuzen in which a cloth tube fitted with a metal tip is used to apply a thin ribbon of rice paste to the outlines of a drawing on the fabric. Dyes of various colours are brushed within the paste boundaries, which prevent bleeding of one colour into another.  This method has been combined with a stencil-dyeing technique called kata kanoko, which imitates tie-dyeing, and embroidery in silk and metallic threads."@en , "Tex, Japan, embroidery"@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/ebcd749c-a187-3d45-bd22-aec2dc4bb321/dimension/2> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/ebcd749c-a187-3d45-bd22-aec2dc4bb321/dimension/1> ;
